<section class="case-section">
  <div class="case-section__head"><span class="mono" data-reveal>Context</span><h2 class="display-s" data-split>A card game that has to earn its place in a pocket.</h2></div>
  <div class="case-section__body prose" data-reveal>
    <p>Pocket Knights is a mobile collectible card game built at Anomaly Games, where I progressed from Associate Game Director to Game Director and led a cross-functional team across design, engineering and art. Mobile is unforgiving: a player gives you a few minutes on a train, and either the game earns tomorrow or it doesn’t.</p>
    <p>So the direction started where the player does. Before the meta, before monetization, before the roadmap — what happens in the first fifteen minutes, and does every system after that keep the promise those minutes made?</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section">
  <div class="case-section__head"><span class="mono" data-reveal>What I did</span><h2 class="display-s" data-split>Onboarding as a system, not a tutorial.</h2></div>
  <div class="case-section__body" data-reveal>
    <ul>
      <li><span><b>First fifteen minutes.</b> Designed the onboarding flow beat by beat — first battle, first card, first meaningful choice, first goal — and used it as the yardstick for every later feature.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Economy &amp; progression.</b> Designed the currency model, capsule and pack design, battle pass cadence and the progression curve that ties them together.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Live ops.</b> Built the event cadence and the live-ops strategy that keeps a small team shipping without burning out.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Simulation.</b> Built MCTS- and CFR-inspired simulation for economy balancing and player-behaviour modelling, so changes were tested against thousands of simulated sessions before a real one.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Studio leadership.</b> Hiring, creative direction and go-to-market strategy across Anomaly’s portfolio — Pocket Knights alongside Don’t Die, Goonville, Gmeow and Moo.F.O.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section">
  <div class="case-section__head"><span class="mono" data-reveal>Direction</span><h2 class="display-s" data-split>Small team. Whole studio.</h2></div>
  <div class="case-section__body prose" data-reveal>
    <p>Directing at a small studio means the vision has to survive contact with a Tuesday. I wrote the design docs, ran the reviews, lived in the analytics, and shipped features myself when that was the fastest way to unblock the team. I also listed what was broken before anyone found it — a habit that holds trust when the pace is high.</p>
    <p>The throughline is the same as every game I have worked on: make the systems fair, make the first session generous, and make the people around you better at the work.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<figure class="case-quote">
  <blockquote data-split>Onboarding is not a tutorial. It is <em>the promise</em> the rest of the game has to keep.</blockquote>
  <figcaption class="mono" data-reveal>Matthew Shera — on directing Pocket Knights</figcaption>
